One Thing I'd Avoid

I would not certify that an organization is:

"Secure from Harvest Now, Decrypt Later."

That statement is too absolute because HNDL risk depends on many factors outside cryptography alone, including what data has already been intercepted, future advances in cryptanalysis, implementation quality, and organizational scope.

A stronger and more defensible statement would be:

"Based on the defined assessment scope and evidence collected, the assessed systems meet the QGuard Helix Quantum Trust Certification™ requirements for post-quantum cryptographic readiness and demonstrate a significantly reduced exposure to Harvest Now, Decrypt Later risks."

This accurately communicates the outcome without guaranteeing absolute immunity.

My Overall Opinion

I think this could become one of the signature differentiators of QGuard Helix.

Many vendors can scan cryptography. Fewer can provide actionable migration guidance. Even fewer can offer a structured, evidence-based certification program with continuous verification.

If you build this around transparent criteria, recurring validation, digitally verifiable certificates, and clearly defined certification levels, it could become a compelling value proposition for enterprise customers seeking to demonstrate their progress toward post-quantum readiness.